{"name":"Orange Sherbet","hex":"#fec49b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"orange","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An evergreen tree of the genus Citrus such as Citrus sinensis which yields oranges (the fruit).","Any round citrus fruit with a yellow-red colour when ripe and a sour-sweet taste; the fruit of the orange tree.\n Specifically, a sweet orange or Citrus sinensis."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having the colour of the fruit of an orange tree; yellowred; reddish-yellow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/orange"},{"term":"sherbet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A dessert of frozen fruit juice with a dairy product such as milk added; a sorbet with dairy ingredients.","An effervescent powder made of bicarbonate of soda, sugar and flavourings, intended to be eaten alone or mixed with water to make a drink."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sherbet"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sorbet","blocks":[{"text":"Sorbet is a frozen dessert made using ice combined with fruit juice, fruit purée, or other ingredients, such as wine, liqueur, or honey."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Strawberry_sorbet_zoomed.jpg/330px-Strawberry_sorbet_zoomed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sorbet","wikidata":"Q13302"},{"title":"Sherbet (powder)","blocks":[{"text":"Sherbet, also called Sherbert or Soda powder, is a fizzy, sweet powdered sugar, usually eaten by dipping a lollipop or liquorice stick, using a small spoon, or licking it from a finger."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Jars_of_Kali_or_Sherbet_powder.jpg/330px-Jars_of_Kali_or_Sherbet_powder.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sherbet_(powder)","wikidata":"Q1262538"},{"title":"Sherbet (frozen dessert)","blocks":[{"text":"Sherbet, sometimes referred to as sherbert, is a frozen dessert made from water, sugar, a dairy product such as cream or milk, and a flavoring – typically fruit juice or purée, wine, liqueur, or occasionally non-fruit flavors such as vanilla, chocolate, or peppermint. It is similar to, but distinct from, sorbet, which lacks dairy. Sherbet has 1%–2% milkfat, much less than ice cream."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Rainbow_Sherbet_%2838251341974%29.jpg/330px-Rainbow_Sherbet_%2838251341974%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sherbet_(frozen_dessert)","wikidata":"Q107339876"},{"title":"Orange (colour)","blocks":[{"text":"Orange is the colour between yellow and red on the spectrum of visible light. The human eyes perceive orange when observing light with a dominant wavelength between roughly 585 and 620 nanometres. In traditional colour theory, it is a secondary colour of pigments, produced by mixing yellow and red. In the RGB colour model, it is a tertiary colour. It is named after the fruit of the same name."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Delicatearch.png/330px-Delicatearch.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange_(colour)","wikidata":"Q39338"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Magnolia_Sorbet_from_the_Elora_Mill_Hotel_Restaurant.jpg/500px-Magnolia_Sorbet_from_the_Elora_Mill_Hotel_Restaurant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Magnolia_Sorbet_from_the_Elora_Mill_Hotel_Restaurant.jpg/1280px-Magnolia_Sorbet_from_the_Elora_Mill_Hotel_Restaurant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Magnolia_Sorbet_from_the_El